-- 1363: Blog post -- "Agentic AI for Video Creation: Building a Production Pipeline, Not Just Faster Edits".
-- Focus: the agentic video pipeline (ideation -> script -> storyboard -> assets -> edit -> QA ->
-- distribute), vibe coding to orchestrate it, and the QA gate that keeps quality up at volume.
-- Funnels to WSQ Agentic AI for Video Creation (TGS-2023036088), SG-only.
-- Published live (status=1), BACK-DATED to 2025-07-20 per the admin's "date all the digital
-- marketing blogs in Apr to Aug 2025" (post 13 of 16, WSQ/CASL digital marketing batch).
--
-- BACK-DATED => linkedin_urn / facebook_post_id = 'manual-skip' so publishDue's share
-- step permanently no-ops (standing rule, blog-pipeline SKILL.md "Hard-won rules").
--
-- All external + course URLs verified HTTP 200 on 2026-09-07.
--
-- NOTE: hero_image_url is NULL here by design -- generate the hero ON PROD after
-- deploy. Hero artwork string (NOT the stored title, which would mis-theme):
--   artwork: AI Video Creation: Script, Edit and Publish at Scale
--   kicker:  Video Creation

-- @mms_instance is pre-set by apply.php from MMS_COUNTRY_CODE (defaults to 'SG').
SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);

INSERT INTO mmd_blog_post
  (title, url_key, excerpt, content, author, status, published_at, related_skus, source_sku,
   meta_title, meta_description, meta_keywords, likes, linkedin_urn, facebook_post_id,
   created_at, updated_at)
SELECT 'Agentic AI for Video Creation: Building a Production Pipeline, Not Just Faster Edits', 'agentic-ai-video-creation-pipeline', CONCAT('Faster editing helps one video. A pipeline helps every video ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' agents that handle ideation, scripting, storyboards, voiceover, asset generation, editing and QA, orchestrated with vibe coding so a marketer can build it without deep programming. Here is how the stages connect, plus the WSQ course funded up to 70%.'),
  '<p><strong>Short version:</strong> Using AI to edit faster improves one video. Building a pipeline improves every video you will ever make. The difference is that a pipeline assigns each stage &mdash; ideation, scripting, storyboarding, voiceover, asset generation, editing, quality assurance, distribution &mdash; to an agent with its own brief and quality bar, and defines what passes between them. The barrier used to be that orchestrating this required real programming; vibe coding lowers that barrier enough for a marketer or a media professional to build and adapt their own. This article covers the stages, the orchestration and the quality gate that keeps volume from degrading craft. The hands-on version is the <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-video-creation.html">WSQ Agentic AI for Video Creation</a> course (TGS-2023036088), funded up to 70%.</p> <h2>Why a pipeline beats a faster editor</h2> <p>Consider a team that adopts AI editing tools and halves its edit time. Good, but the video still waits on someone to have the idea, someone to write the script, someone to source the b-roll and someone to check the result. The edit was never the only bottleneck; it was just the most visible one. Halving one stage in a seven-stage chain changes total throughput far less than people expect.</p> <p>A pipeline attacks every stage at once, and it does something a faster editor cannot: it makes the process <em>repeatable</em>. The tenth video follows the same path as the first, which is what allows quality to hold as volume rises. That is the actual promise here &mdash; not speed, but consistency at speed.</p> <h2>The stages, and what each agent needs</h2> <table> <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Agent produces</th><th>Needs to be given</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Ideation</td><td>Concepts matched to the campaign and audience</td><td>The content plan, past performance, the audience brief</td></tr> <tr><td>Scripting</td><td>A timed script written for the ear, with b-roll notes</td><td>The chosen concept, voice guide, length target</td></tr> <tr><td>Storyboarding</td><td>Shot-by-shot plan, visual references</td><td>The script, house visual style</td></tr> <tr><td>Voiceover</td><td>Narration track at the right pace</td><td>Script, approved voice (with consent if cloned)</td></tr> <tr><td>Asset generation</td><td>Images, b-roll, motion graphics, music</td><td>Storyboard, brand palette and type</td></tr> <tr><td>Editing</td><td>Assembled cut against the storyboard</td><td>All assets, pacing notes</td></tr> <tr><td>Quality assurance</td><td>A list of defects and claim issues</td><td>The QA checklist &mdash; see below</td></tr> <tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Platform variants, captions, metadata</td><td>The approved cut, channel specs</td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>The right-hand column is where most pipelines are won or lost. An agent given a vague brief produces vague output at every stage, and by the time it reaches the edit nobody can tell where the problem started.</p> <h2>Vibe coding: orchestration without becoming a developer</h2> <p>Connecting these stages used to mean writing real integration code, which put pipelines out of reach for the people who actually make the videos. Vibe coding &mdash; describing the workflow you want in plain language and iterating on what comes back &mdash; changes who can build one. You describe the stage, what it receives, what it must produce and what should happen when it fails, then refine as you test.</p> <p>The discipline that makes this work is treating each stage as replaceable. Keep the interfaces between stages simple and explicit, and you can swap a voice tool or an image generator without rebuilding the pipeline. Teams that let stages reach into each other''s internals end up with something that breaks whenever a tool updates.</p> <h2>The QA gate: the stage nobody wants and everybody needs</h2> <p>Volume without a quality gate produces a lot of mediocre video quickly, which damages a brand faster than publishing nothing. The QA agent should check mechanically verifiable things and hand the rest to a person:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Technical:</strong> audio levels consistent, no clipping, captions present and synced, correct aspect ratio and duration per platform, brand assets used correctly.</li> <li><strong>Content:</strong> claims traceable to the brief, no invented statistics, names and figures verified, disclosures present where required.</li> <li><strong>Consistency:</strong> voice, palette, typography and lower-thirds matching the house style.</li> </ul> <p>What it cannot judge is whether the video is any good &mdash; whether the hook works, whether the pacing holds, whether it feels like your brand. That is a human review and it should be short and final: one person, one pass, publish or send back.</p> <h2>Start with the two stages that hurt most</h2> <p>Building all eight stages before producing anything is the classic way to spend a month on plumbing. The faster route is to find your own bottleneck and automate that pair first.</p> <ul> <li><strong>If videos stall before they start,</strong> your bottleneck is ideation and scripting. Automate those two and you will have scripts waiting rather than a blank calendar.</li> <li><strong>If footage sits unedited,</strong> automate asset generation and the assembly step, so a shoot becomes a rough cut the same day rather than next month.</li> <li><strong>If quality is inconsistent,</strong> build the QA gate first, before anything else. It will tell you which stage is actually failing, which is often not the one you assumed.</li> </ul> <p>Whichever pair you pick, run it on real work for a fortnight before adding a third stage. The instructions always need adjusting on contact with your own material, and fixing two stages is tractable in a way that debugging eight at once is not.</p> <h2>Rights, consent and honesty</h2> <p>Generated video raises questions that a faster edit did not. Three rules worth writing into the pipeline itself:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Consent for any cloned voice or likeness.</strong> Your own, or documented permission. This is not negotiable and should be a QA checklist item, not a memory.</li> <li><strong>No fabricated proof.</strong> Synthetic testimonials, invented results and staged &ldquo;customer&rdquo; footage presented as real fall foul of <a href="https://asas.org.sg/">Singapore advertising standards</a> regardless of how they were produced.</li> <li><strong>Keep provenance.</strong> Record which assets were generated and from what, so a question months later has an answer.</li> </ol> <p>Where personal data appears in footage or targeting, the <a href="https://www.pdpc.gov.sg/overview-of-pdpa/the-legislation/personal-data-protection-act">PDPA</a> applies as it would to any other marketing asset.</p> <h2>What throughput realistically looks like</h2> <p>A small team running a working pipeline typically moves from one or two videos a month to one or two a week, with the short-form variants coming almost free because distribution is a pipeline stage rather than an afterthought. The gain is not evenly distributed across stages: ideation and scripting speed up the most, editing moderately, and the human review stays roughly constant &mdash; which is fine, because that review is the thing protecting quality.</p> <p>We documented a working example of this pattern, with agents coordinated on a kanban board, in our post on <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hermes-agent-kanban-multi-agent-video-team">running a multi-agent video team</a>.</p> <h2>Learn it hands-on, funded up to 70%</h2> <p>The <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-video-creation.html">WSQ &ndash; Agentic AI for Video Creation</a> course (TGS-2023036088) is a two-day programme for media professionals, content creators, marketers and digital storytellers. Its three topics:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Creative strategy and end-to-end video production with agentic AI</strong> &mdash; ideation, scripting, storyboards, voiceover, image and video asset generation.</li> <li><strong>AI-assisted video editing, storytelling and quality assurance</strong> &mdash; editing, post-production and the QA gate.</li> <li><strong>Workflow optimisation, industry compliance and emerging video technologies</strong> &mdash; orchestrating agents, automating repetitive production tasks and scaling operations.</li> </ol> <p>Vibe coding techniques are used throughout so learners can build and customise agentic workflows without extensive programming experience. Assessment is a written and a practical exam.</p> <table> <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Funding</th><th>What you pay (incl. 9% GST)</th></tr></thead> <tbody> <tr><td>Full course fee</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>S$750.00 before GST (S$67.50 GST)</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above</td><td>50% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$442.50</strong></td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored SG/PR staff</td><td>70% WSQ funding</td><td><strong>S$292.50</strong></td></tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Eligible Singaporeans can offset the nett fee with <a href="https://www.skillsfuture.gov.sg/">SkillsFuture Credit</a> or PSEA, and eligible Singapore-registered companies can tap SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit (SFEC); SME employers can also claim absentee payroll. The course runs over two full days (9:30am to 6:30pm), with physical classroom and synchronous online (Zoom) options. Fees are as published on the course page at the time of writing &mdash; the live page always carries the current fee and the next available dates.</p> <p>If you want the tool-level foundation first, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-creating-engaging-videos-with-generative-ai-genai.html">WSQ Creating Engaging Videos with Generative AI</a> covers scripting, footage improvement and AI post-production; <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/multi-ai-agents-workflow-for-content-creation.html">WSQ Multi AI Agents Workflow for Content Creation</a> applies the same pipeline thinking to written content.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What is the difference between this and the generative AI video course?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>The generative AI course teaches the tools &mdash; scripting, footage improvement, object removal, voice and music &mdash; and makes individual videos much faster. This one teaches how to connect those stages into a repeatable pipeline run by agents, which is what holds quality steady as volume rises.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What is vibe coding and do I need to be a developer?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Vibe coding means describing the workflow you want in plain language and iterating on what comes back, rather than writing integration code from scratch. It is how a marketer or media professional can build and adapt an agentic pipeline without a programming background, which is exactly what the course assumes.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does a video pipeline reduce quality?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Only if you skip the QA gate. Volume without quality control produces mediocre video faster, which is worse than publishing less. Automate the mechanical checks &mdash; audio, captions, aspect ratio, claim traceability, brand consistency &mdash; and keep one short, final human review for whether it is actually good.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Can I use a cloned voice in marketing videos?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>With explicit consent from the person whose voice it is. Your own voice is fine; anyone else&rsquo;s requires documented permission. Make it a QA checklist item rather than something you rely on remembering, and never fabricate testimonials or results.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How much more video can a small team realistically produce?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Moving from one or two videos a month to one or two a week is typical, with short-form variants coming almost free because distribution becomes a pipeline stage. Ideation and scripting speed up most; human review stays roughly constant, which is what protects quality.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What funding applies to this course?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>TGS-2023036088 is WSQ funded at 50% for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above and 70% for Singaporeans aged 40 and above or SME-sponsored staff, with SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and SFEC available against the nett fee.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>The bottom line</h2> <p>A faster edit is a local improvement; a pipeline is a structural one. Give each stage an agent with a real brief, keep the interfaces between stages simple enough to swap a tool, put a mechanical QA gate before the human review, and write the consent and honesty rules into the checklist rather than trusting memory. That is what turns AI video from an occasional trick into a production capability.</p> <p><strong>Ready to build the pipeline?</strong> <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-agentic-ai-for-video-creation.html">Register for WSQ &ndash; Agentic AI for Video Creation</a> &mdash; 2 days, funded up to 70%, SkillsFuture Credit claimable.</p>',
  'Dr. Alfred Ang', 1, '2025-07-20', 'TGS-2023036088', 'TGS-2023036088',
  'Agentic AI for Video Creation: An End-to-End Pipeline | WSQ Course Singapore',
  CONCAT('Build an agentic video production pipeline ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' ideation, scripting, storyboards, voiceover, asset generation, editing, QA and distribution orchestrated with vibe coding ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' with the WSQ Agentic AI for Video Creation course (TGS-2023036088), funded up to 70%.'),
  'agentic ai video creation, ai video pipeline, vibe coding video workflow, ai video agents, automated video production, video qa ai, wsq video creation course, skillsfuture video course, ai storyboard, ai voiceover workflow, content production automation, video workflow orchestration',
  122, 'manual-skip', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW()
FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg = 1
  AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT post_id FROM mmd_blog_post WHERE url_key = 'agentic-ai-video-creation-pipeline') x);

